.TH PORTBRIDGE 1 "2026" "portbridge 2.0.0" "PortBridge Manual"
.SH NAME
portbridge \- manage TCP port forwarding to the public internet through your own relay
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B portbridge
.RI [ command ]
.RI [ options ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B portbridge
exposes a TCP service listening on this Linux machine to ordinary internet
TCP clients, without requiring the remote machine to install any VPN
client, special software, or TLS wrapper. It does this by running
.BR frpc (1)
(the client half of
.IR frp ,
Fast Reverse Proxy) as a managed subprocess: frpc connects
.I outbound
to a relay server (frps) you deploy yourself on any host with one public
TCP port. Only this machine needs frpc installed; a remote user connects
with a plain TCP client such as
.BR nc (1).
.PP
portbridge never inspects, modifies, or executes anything received over
the forwarded connection \(em it forwards raw TCP bytes only.
.PP
Deploying the relay itself is a one\-time step outside portbridge \(em see
the README for a worked Dockerfile/frps.toml example. portbridge manages
the client side only: installing frpc, generating its config, running and
supervising it.
.PP
With no command, portbridge launches an interactive menu.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B setup
Check whether frpc is installed and the relay is configured/reachable \(em
offering to download frpc for this machine's CPU architecture, and
walking through entering the relay's address/ports/token if missing.
Never proceeds on its own in a non\-interactive context unless
.B \-\-yes
is given.
.RS
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Assume yes on all confirmation prompts.
.RE
.TP
.B start
Start TCP port forwarding. Prompts interactively for anything not given on
the command line.
.RS
.TP
.BR \-\-port =\fIPORT\fR
Local port to forward.
.TP
.BR \-\-bind =\fIADDRESS\fR
Local bind address (default 127.0.0.1).
.TP
.B \-\-foreground
Run in the foreground instead of detaching a background monitor. Intended
for use as a systemd
.B ExecStart
command.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Assume yes on all confirmation prompts (for scripting).
.RE
.TP
.B stop
Stop TCP port forwarding started by portbridge. Terminates the monitor
process, which cleanly stops its frpc child as part of shutdown. Never
affects the local service listening on the forwarded port, and never
affects processes portbridge did not start.
.TP
.B status
Print a detailed status report: frpc install state, relay
configured/reachable, whether the local port is listening, current
forwarding status, the public endpoint, uptime, and a health checklist.
.TP
.B endpoint
Print the current public endpoint as
.IR host:port .
.TP
.B test
Test connectivity.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-local
Test only the local port.
.TP
.B \-\-external
Test only the external (public) endpoint.
.TP
.BR \-\-port =\fIPORT\fR
Override which local port to test.
.RE
.TP
.B logs
Show recent log entries from
.IR ~/.local/state/portbridge/portbridge.log ,
including frpc's own log output.
.RS
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-follow
Follow the log live (like
.BR "tail \-f" ).
.TP
.BR \-n =\fIN\fR
Number of lines to show (default 50).
.RE
.TP
.B restart
Stop (if running) and start again using the previously stored
configuration.
.TP
.B configure
Interactive configuration wizard, or pass flags (see
.BR "portbridge configure \-\-help" )
to set specific values non\-interactively, including
.B \-\-relay\-token
to set the relay auth token (stored outside config.toml).
.TP
.B listen
Run a disposable TCP echo listener for testing, completely separate from
forwarding management. Equivalent in spirit to
.BR "nc \-l \-p PORT" .
Requires
.BR \-\-port .
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the installed version and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/portbridge/config.toml
User configuration (bind address, local port, relay address/ports,
health\-check/backoff timings, logging level). Contains no secrets.
.TP
.I ~/.config/portbridge/relay_token
The relay auth token, mode 600. The one real secret portbridge holds.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/portbridge/state.json
Current runtime state: whether forwarding is active, the monitor PID,
the local port, the public address/port, start time, and health.
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/portbridge/frpc.generated.toml
frpc's config, generated fresh each time forwarding starts. Mode 600
(contains the auth token).
.TP
.I ~/.local/state/portbridge/portbridge.log
Rotating log file.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B 0
on success,
.B 1
on a handled error (message printed to stderr),
.B 130
if interrupted with Ctrl+C.
.SH SECURITY
portbridge keeps the relay auth token out of config.toml, in a mode\-600
file, and only ever reads it to generate frpc's own (also mode\-600)
config. It never scans the network, never opens ports you did not
request, never modifies local firewall rules (frpc only makes outbound
connections, so none are needed), and never executes anything received
over the forwarded TCP connection.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR nc (1),
.BR systemctl (1)
.br
frp project: https://github.com/fatedier/frp
